  "headline": "Oil-led inflation pulse is being priced as a rate story, not a growth scare",
  "body": [
    "Brent up 43% over three months to about $103, the 10-year yield up 79 basis points over the same span to 5.28% and the dollar index a hair off its 52-week high: markets are pricing an energy-led inflation pulse and a tighter policy response to it. They are not pricing a growth scare. Copper is up 17% year to date near the top of its range, the copper/gold ratio rose 7% over the month and bitcoin is up 38% in three months.",
    "The curve confirms the read. The 30-year minus 10-year spread narrowed 12 basis points over the month to 35, with the 10-year rising 48 points against 36 at the long end — a bear flattening, in which the market expects policy to lean against inflation rather than lose control of it. Gold fits: down 5.5% on the month as yields rose, it is real rates biting, with no fear bid. One bitcoin now buys 20 ounces of gold, 16% more than a month ago; that is liquidity and appetite, not hedging.",
    "The tension is the week and the dollar. The index is up 2.4% over the month, so part of gold's and copper's dollar-priced slide is currency; USD/CAD at a 52-week high with crude above $100 shows dollar demand overriding the oil windfall. Copper down 3% this week alongside gold is the first hint the pulse is pinching. The three-month trend outweighs one week, but the strain is real."
  ],
